Slides (Title, Slide text, and Speaker notes):

Slide 1 — Title & Opening
Title: Electric Vehicle (EV) Ecosystem Development in Ghana: Infrastructure, Policy & Investment Opportunities
Subtitle: Practical pathways to a reliable 24‑hour economy
Speaker notes:
- "Good morning. I’m Rev. Engr. Dr. Frimpong Kyeremeh. Today I’ll show practical, implementable ways Ghana can build an EV ecosystem that supports a 24‑hour economy without destabilizing the grid."

Slide 2 — Problem statement: new demand pattern
Key points:
- Electrification of transport shifts energy demand to new times (nightly passenger charging, episodic freight charging).
- Risks: transformer overloads, voltage issues, higher losses and cost-of-service.
Speaker notes:
- Provide the neighbourhood transformer example (250 kVA with 20x 7 kW chargers → 140 kW added).

Slide 3 — What the EV ecosystem includes
Bullets: vehicles, chargers (AC/DC), grid & distribution, BESS, software & comms, standards & cybersecurity, business & finance, policy.
Speaker notes:
- Analogy: city water system — pipes, pumps, tanks and billing.

Slide 4 — Technical building blocks & how they work together
Bullets: ITS (routing & scheduling), Smart Grid integration (AMI, managed charging), Charging networks (AC/DC), BESS sizing, Interconnection (IEEE 1547).
Ghana examples:
- Logistics park scheduler reduces peak draw by ~40% vs FCFS.
- Medium hub: 4x150 kW DCFC = 600 kW peak; limit on-grid to 350 kW with ~350 kWh BESS.
Speaker notes:
- Explain telemetry & reservation integration; emphasize local decision logic for failsafe.

Slide 5 — Practical Ghana pilots
Pilot 1 — Logistics (Tema–Accra corridor): 4x150 kW DCFC + 350 kWh BESS + 150 kW PV. Objective: scheduling integration with fleet telematics.
Pilot 2 — Residential (Accra): 50-unit development, 10x7 kW wallboxes, managed charging.
Pilot 3 — Commercial (Mall): 10x22 kW AC + 2x60 kW DCFC + 200 kWh BESS + rooftop PV.
Speaker notes:
- Describe anchor tenants, KPIs (uptime, utilization), procurement and O&M contracts.

Slide 6 — Investment opportunities & business models
Opportunities: charger install & O&M, BESS+PV financing (ESAs), CPMS & telematics, local assembly & training.
Revenue examples: Mall pilot ~720 kWh/day → ~262,800 kWh/yr → revenue at $0.10/kWh ≈ $26k/yr (energy only).
Speaker notes:
- Highlight subscription plans for fleets and revenue-share with landlords.

Slide 7 — Policy & regulatory asks
1) Update building code: EV‑ready wiring in new builds from 2027.
2) Recommend OCPP & ISO 15118 for procurement.
3) Pilot co-financing window for bundled chargers+PV+BESS.
4) Clear cost-sharing rules for grid upgrades.
Speaker notes:
- Stress simple, time-bound asks to unlock private capital.

Slide 8 — Standards, safety & cybersecurity
Standards: IEC 61851, IEC 62196, ISO 15118, OCPP, IEEE 1547.
Safety: BESS fire planning, earthing, emergency isolation.
Cybersecurity: TLS mutual auth, signed firmware, network segmentation.
Speaker notes:
- Recommend procurement include cybersecurity & safety clauses.

Slide 9 — KPIs, metrics & roadmap
KPIs: charger availability (>98% DCFC), utilization, peak draw reduction %, MTTR <24 hours urban.
Roadmap: Phase 0 (0–6 months) policy & standards; Phase 1 (6–18 months) pilots; Phase 2 (18–48 months) scale; Phase 3 (48+ months) market maturity & V2G where viable.
Speaker notes:
- Emphasize starting where utilization is secured: logistics & malls.

Slide 10 — Call to action
Asks: support three pilots on Tema–Accra corridor; adopt EV‑ready building codes; create pilot co-finance window.
Investor pitch: fund bundled PV+BESS+charger pilot with anchor tenant fleet for predictable utilization.
Speaker notes:
- Closing: "Let’s start three pilots, learn fast, and scale with clear rules that share costs sensibly. Ghana can lead West Africa in reliable EV infrastructure." 

Backup slides — worked math
Example A — Residential (50-unit, 10 chargers): 10x7 kW chargers: if each charges 4 hours/day => 10 * 7 * 4 = 280 kWh/day.
Transformer check: 250 kVA nominal — require load management to keep additional evening peak <50 kW.
Example B — Logistics hub (4x150 kW DCFC): Peak instantaneous = 600 kW. If on-grid draw limited to 350 kW, BESS must supply 250 kW for 1 hour => 250 kWh usable -> choose 350 kWh BESS with margin.
Example C — Mall (12x22 kW AC + 2x60 kW DCFC): Max draw = (12*22) + (2*60) = 384 kW. 200 kWh BESS recommended for demand charge mitigation.

